), Changbai Road, Changping District, Beijing, 102206, China\n\n   Qian Ren, Si-Han Li, Shi-Jian Zhou, Di Mu, Yu Li, Qiu-Lan Chen & Yan-Ping Zhang\n2. Chinese Field Epidemiology Training Program, Chinese Center for Disease Control and Prevention (Chinese Academy of Preventive Medicine), Beijing, China\n\n   Qian Ren\n3. School of Public Health, Guangxi Medical University, Nanning, China\n\n   Hui-Jie Qin & Jun-Yuan Chen\n\nAuthors\n\n1. Qian Ren\n\n   View author publications\n\n   Search author on:PubMed Google Scholar\n2. Hui-Jie Qin\n\n   View author publications\n\n   Search author on:PubMed Google Scholar\n3. Si-Han Li\n\n   View author publications\n\n   Search author on:PubMed Google Scholar\n4. Shi-Jian Zhou\n\n   View author publications\n\n   Search author on:PubMed Google Scholar\n5. Jun-Yuan Chen\n\n   View author publications\n\n   Search author on:PubMed Google Scholar\n6. Di Mu\n\n   View author publications\n\n   Search author on:PubMed Google Scholar\n7. Yu Li\n\n   View author publications\n\n   Search author on:PubMed Google Scholar\n8. Qiu-Lan Chen\n\n   View author publications\n\n   Search author on:PubMed Google Scholar\n9. Yan-Ping Zhang\n\n   View author publications\n\n   Search author on:PubMed Google Scholar\n\n### Contributions\n\nQR and HJ-Q were responsible for data analysis and drafting the initial manuscript. SH-L, SJ-Z, and JY-C handled data collection and cleaning. DM and YL offered technical guidance. YP-Z conceived and designed the study. QL-C and YP-Z supervised the research and revised the manuscript. YP-Z served as corresponding author. All authors approved the final manuscript version.\n\n### Corresponding author\n\nCorrespondence to\nYan-Ping Zhang.\n\n## Ethics declarations\n\n### Ethics approval and consent to participate\n\nThis study was a retrospective epidemiological analysis based entirely on existing surveillance records from the China Information System for Disease Control and Prevention (CISDCP), the national mandatory reporting system for notifiable infectious diseases. The data used were fully anonymized at the source, containing no personally identifiable information. No human experiments or human tissues were collected. According to the Measures for Ethical Review of Life Sciences and Medical Research Involving Human Subjects (Jointly issued by the National Health Commission of China and other ministries, 2023), research activities that solely involve analysis of anonymized, routinely collected public health surveillance data are exempt from formal ethical review board approval. The need for individual informed consent was waived because the study involved no direct contact with participants, which is consistent with the provisions of the Declaration of Helsinki for research using pre-existing, anonymized data. All methods were performed in accordance with relevant guidelines and regulations, including the Declaration of Helsinki. This activity was conducted as part of the routine disease control mandate of the Chinese Center for Disease Control and Prevention and was determined to be exempt from institutional review board approval according to national regulations (the Measures for Ethical Review of Life Sciences and Medical Research Involving Human Subjects), without requiring a specific ethics committee reference number.\n\n### Consent for publication\n\nNot applicable.\n\n### Competing interests\n\nThe authors declare no competing interests.\n\n## Additional information\n\n### Publisher’s note\n\nSpringer Nature remains neutral with regard to jurisdictional claims in published maps and institutional affiliations.\n\n## Rights and permissions\n\n**Open Access** This article is licensed under a Creative Commons Attribution-NonCommercial-NoDerivatives 4.0 International License, which permits any non-commercial use, sharing, distribution and reproduction in any medium or format, as long as you give appropriate credit to the original author(s) and the source, provide a link to the Creative Commons licence, and indicate if you modified the licensed material. You do not have permission under this licence to share adapted material derived from this article or parts of it. The images or other third party material in this article are included in the article’s Creative Commons licence, unless indicated otherwise in a credit line to the material. If material is not included in the article’s Creative Commons licence and your intended use is not permitted by statutory regulation or exceeds the permitted use, you will need to obtain permission directly from the copyright holder. To view a copy of this licence, visit http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/.\n\nReprints and permissions\n\n## About this article\n\n### Cite this article\n\nRen, Q., Qin, HJ., Li, SH. *et al.* Epidemiological characteristics of severe fever with thrombocytopenia syndrome (SFTS) in China, 2010–2024: a fifteen-year national surveillance analysis.\n*BMC Infect Dis* **26**, 1497 (2026). https://doi.org/10.1186/s12879-026-14066-z\n\nDownload citation\n\n* Received: 25 May 2026\n* Accepted: 20 July 2026\n* Published: 28 July 2026\n* Version of record: 14 August 2026\n* DOI: https://doi.org/10.1186/s12879-026-14066-z\n\n### Share this article\n\nAnyone you share the following link with will be able to read this content:\n\nSorry, a shareable link is not currently available for this article.\n\nProvided by the Springer Nature SharedIt content-sharing initiative\n\n### Keywords\n\n### Profiles\n\n1. Qian Ren\n\n\n   View author profile","author":null,"published_date":"2026-07-28","latency_ms":2002.4762800021563,"format":"markdown"}],"errors":[]}